## Entitlements

Entitlements gate what an item grants its subscribers, and are modelled by two
resources that work together:

- [`chargebee_feature`](resources/feature) defines a capability once at the site
  level. Its `type` controls how the value is read: `switch` (boolean, the
  default), `quantity` (a numeric allowance, pair with a `unit`), `range` (a
  numeric value within a range), or `custom` (free-form). Features created by
  this provider are activated automatically.
- [`chargebee_item_entitlement`](resources/item_entitlement) grants one feature
  to one item and sets the per-item `value`. Its `item_type` must match the
  item's `type`. The upsert is idempotent — an entitlement already present in
  Chargebee is adopted into state and reconciled to your configuration on the
  first apply.

## Stability

This provider follows [semantic versioning](https://semver.org/):

- Resource and data-source schemas are stable within a major version;
  breaking changes only land in a new major version.
- Deprecated attributes keep working for at least six months (and never less
  than one minor release) before removal, and are announced in the changelog
  under BACKWARDS INCOMPATIBILITIES.
- State upgrades are shipped with any schema change that requires them.

## Destroy semantics

Chargebee refuses to delete catalog objects that are in use by subscriptions
or invoices. On destroy this provider:

1. Attempts a real delete.
2. If Chargebee refuses, archives the object instead (items, item prices, and
   features), emits a warning, and removes it from state. Archived ids stay
   reserved in Chargebee.
3. Item families cannot be archived: an in-use family is left in place with a
   warning and removed from state.
4. Item entitlements are revoked directly; there is no archived state for
   them.

